WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.980
A requirement, a product claim and a roadmap answer different questions.

2
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.080
A requirement states what a scoped design must meet.

3
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:11.040
A test report describes observed behavior under particular conditions.

4
00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.800
A roadmap describes intended future work.

5
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.420
None should silently substitute for another.

6
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.160
The same discipline applies to recognition.

7
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.680
Use the precise status recorded for the named product in the O

8
00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.880
C P Marketplace or contribution record.

9
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.880
Don't turn recognition into a blanket certification of every possible rack configuration

10
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.040
or supplier combination.

11
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.320
Check what was actually reviewed and what remains the integrator's responsibility.

12
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.900
Summit material is especially useful for understanding emerging constraints and engineering approaches.

13
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.040
We'll use twenty twenty-six examples, but distinguish published contributions from reference designs,

14
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:47.900
prototypes and future plans.

15
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.860
When a result matters to acceptance, ask for the current design revision,

16
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.620
test configuration, boundary conditions and outstanding limitations.

17
00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.260
That information makes the evidence actionable.

18
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:03.100
A compelling diagram or a large headline rating alone doesn't establish readiness

19
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:03.800
for your deployment.

